schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Tokens for Real-World Assets
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Introduces real-world-asset (RWA) tokenization: turning off-chain valuable
    things (real estate, gold, bonds, art, carbon credits) into blockchain
    tokens that prove ownership and can be fractionally traded. Covers the
    claimed benefits (liquidity, fractional access, transparency, lower cost,
    global access), the real challenges (legal complexity across borders,
    valuation/oracle dependence, custody of the physical asset, scalability),
    the actors involved (issuers, platforms, liquidity providers, service
    providers), and beginner action items.
  audience: newcomers exploring what asset tokenization is and whether to look closer (per source, "target learner Maya")
  known_tensions:
    - blockchain-token-portability vs national-legal-jurisdiction-over-the-underlying-asset
    - claimed-transparency-and-lower-fraud vs continued-dependence-on-a-human-custodian-for-the-physical-asset
  related_concepts:
    - real-world-asset-tokenization
    - fractional-ownership
  related_resources:
    - Centrifuge
    - Polymath
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/tokens-real-world-assets.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: tokens-real-world-assets.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - no people/community profiles, exact locations, TEK, or specific
      funding/legal recommendations named in this article beyond generic
      "watch your country's regs" advice; no high-risk trigger identified.
    - see the accompanying signal object flagging this source document's low
      metadata/citation quality (frontmatter has placeholder/garbage
      "sources: [l, i, s, t]" and no real audience/target_audience array).
